  "fabric": {
    "capacityMbps": 216000,
    "kind": "switching fabric",
    "note": "Vendor switching capacity of 216 Gbps, the full-duplex aggregate of the port rates (48 x 1G + 2 x 10G + 4 x 10G = 108 Gbps each way)."
  },
  "notes": "48-port PoE model: 40 ports of 802.3at and 10 of 802.3bt, the two multi-gig uplinks included in the 60 W group. Switching capacity 216 Gbps is the full-duplex aggregate of the port rates (48 x 1G + 2 x 10G + 4 x 10G = 108 Gbps each way), so it caps nothing the ports cannot already do and is recorded as a fabric figure rather than a limit. Forwarding rate 161 Mpps, 2 MB packet buffer, 32 K MAC addresses, 9 K jumbo frames, 64 static routes, 32 IP interfaces, 4 K VLANs, 64 MB flash and 1 GB RAM. The 600 W and 960 W variants share the same 54-port layout and the same 802.3at/802.3bt split; only the budget differs, which is exactly what the -54HP and -54FP suffixes state. Physical stacking is a datasheet feature rather than a modelled one: the English datasheet says a stack is built through the last two or four 10-Gigabit fibre ports, up to four units in a ring, while the specification table in the same document still lists physical stacking as being in a future firmware release - recorded here because two pages of one datasheet disagree about whether the shipped firmware already carries it. The console port is USB Type-C rather than RJ-45, and the User Guide (https://download.zyxel.com/XGS2220-54FP/user_guide/XGS2220-54FP-UG.pdf) describes that connector as troubleshooting-only until the Access L3 licence is activated, after which it carries the CLI. Any serial-console assumption about this box is wrong twice over: the connector, and the condition. 1RU, 441 x 270 x 44 mm, single fixed AC input (100-240 V AC), smart fan. Sources: datasheet https://www.zyxel.kr/support/datasheet/XGS2220_Series.pdf and the product page https://www.zyxel.com/global/en/products/switch/xgs2220-series/specifications.",
  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 600,
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.zyxel.kr/support/datasheet/XGS2220_Series.pdf",
    "note": "600 W system budget across 50 PoE ports: 802.3at (30 W) on ports 1-40, 802.3bt (60 W) on ports 41-48 and on multi-gig ports 49-50. Per-port maxima sum to 40 x 30 + 10 x 60 = 1800 W against a 600 W budget, so the budget binds long before the ports do - the same relationship as the -30HP, at three times the copper count."
  }
}
